---
import CaseStudy from '../../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro'
import MonitorDemo from '../../../demos/monitor/MonitorDemo'
---

<CaseStudy slug="product-monitor">
  <div class="stats" slot="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="value">~20s</div><div class="label">from release to alert</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="value">~20</div><div class="label">tables in its SQLite database</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="value">~970</div><div class="label">automated tests</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="value">24/7</div><div class="label">running since June 2025</div></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why I built it</h2>
  <p>
    A few Discord communities I belong to collect merch from two online stores. Popular items sell out quickly,
    and people kept missing releases because they were at work or asleep when something went up. I wanted
    everyone to hear about new items and restocks as soon as they happened.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Posts new products, restocks and sell-outs to whichever channels each server picks.</li>
    <li>Each alert shows the price, the sizes or versions available, and a link to the product.</li>
    <li>
      Members can react to an alert to get pinged the next time that product line has news, so nobody has to
      watch the channel all day.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it’s built</h2>
  <p>
    The bot is written in Python. At first it kept everything in JSON files, which got harder to manage as more
    servers joined. I moved it to a SQLite database with about 20 related tables covering products, variants,
    server settings, subscriptions, and every alert sent. Before switching over, the migration compared record
    counts table by table to make sure nothing was lost.
  </p>
  <p>
    It runs on a small Linux server. A health check restarts the bot if it ever stops responding, and the
    database is backed up to AWS S3 during the day. Once a month an automated job restores the newest backup
    and checks that it opens cleanly. About 970 automated tests cover the database, the alert formatting, and
    each server’s settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the data</h2>
  <p>
    The bot records when each product first shows up on each page of the store. After a couple of weeks I had
    enough history to ask which page lists new items first. I expected the main product listing to win. The
    sitemap did, usually within seconds, and some pages I’d have guessed were faster took close to half an
    hour. I reworked the bot to check the sitemap first, and alerts now go out in about 20 seconds.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same data answers the questions the communities ask me, like how quickly limited items sell out and
    how long restocks usually take. You can run those queries yourself below.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    If I’d gone with my first guess, I would have spent a lot of effort on the wrong page. Now, when a design depends on an assumption like that, I try to test it with data first.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="demo">
    <h2 id="demo">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      The demo uses two weeks of made-up data from two made-up stores and a trimmed-down copy of the bot’s database, all running in your browser. The first tab shows alerts the way they look in Discord. The second lets you query
      the data, and you can edit the SQL or write your own.
    </p>
    <div class="demo-frame">
      <MonitorDemo client:visible />
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
